o this is a good illustration of what a  good vantage point  this castle has over the whole area  these modern bailey castles were usually  made of wood as they could be  constructed fairly quickly  with relatively low skilled workers  much later in around about 11 50  stone cladding was being added to the  mound  part of the site to improve the  durability  of the area by the early 1200s  it was no longer required and gradually  fell into disuse  the castle itself may look small to our  eyes  but to the local population it was  massive  they only lived in small thatched  buildings  so this large construction was totally  alien to them  as the castling railing fell into  decline  the local lord began to construct  a newcastle at hadley overlooking the  thames estuary  eventually in 1394  richard ii gave permission for the  townspeople of rayleigh to use the stone  in the site  and it became farmland for the next 500  years  by the early 1900s the site was saved  for future generations by its donation  to the national trust
 

